\title{Variance estimation by selection method}
\description{
Which variance estimator each selection method reaches at export, and how
exact it is. Selection support and variance support are separate
contracts. Every method in \code{?selection-methods} draws a sample, but not
every one has an exact variance estimator in the survey framework.
}
\details{
A sample reaches \code{survey} in three ways. \code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} linearizes,
with Brewer's approximation for PPS stages unless \code{pps} is given. For a
single-stage sample, a joint-probability matrix from \code{\link[=joint_expectation]{joint_expectation()}}
passed as \code{pps = survey::ppsmat()} replaces that approximation.
\code{\link[=as_svrepdesign]{as_svrepdesign()}} builds replicate weights through \code{survey}'s generic
types or svrep's Rao-Wu-Yue-Beaumont (RWYB) bootstrap.
\code{\link[=variance_estimators]{variance_estimators()}} reports, before anything is drawn, which
estimators a design supports, which only approximately, and which the
export would refuse.
\subsection{Method families and their variance estimators}{\tabular{llll}{
   Method or family \tab First-order quantity \tab Joint information \tab Variance estimation \cr
   \code{srswor} \tab Exact inclusion probabilities \tab SRS formulas (not exposed by the joint helper) \tab SRS with FPC, standard replicates or RWYB \cr
   \code{srswr}, \code{pps_multinomial} \tab Exact expected hit counts \tab Exact joint hits for PPS \tab WR draw occurrences with standard replicates or RWYB \cr
   \code{systematic} \tab Exact inclusion probabilities \tab Not exposed by the joint helper \tab SRS approximation under \code{systematic_variance}, or generic replicates \cr
   \code{pps_systematic} \tab Exact inclusion probabilities \tab Exact order-specific matrix (zero pairs may occur) \tab Brewer's approximation under \code{systematic_variance}, a joint matrix, or generic replicates \cr
   \code{bernoulli}, \code{pps_poisson} \tab Exact independent probabilities \tab Exact Poisson matrix for PPS \tab Analytic single-stage Poisson variance, or RWYB for supported clustered/multistage designs \cr
   \code{pps_brewer}, \code{pps_cps}, \code{pps_sampford} \tab Exact inclusion probabilities \tab Approximate for Brewer, exact for CPS/Sampford \tab Brewer by default, an explicit joint matrix, or PPS-compatible replicates (including approximate RWYB) \cr
   \code{pps_sps}, \code{pps_pareto} \tab Approximate targets \tab High-entropy approximation using targets \tab Brewer or generic PPS-compatible replicates (no built-in RWYB mapping) \cr
   \code{pps_chromy} \tab Exact expected hit counts \tab Monte Carlo joint hits \tab WR or generic replicate approximation to PMR (no built-in RWYB mapping) \cr
   Unconstrained \code{cube} \tab Exact inclusion probabilities under the method contract \tab High-entropy approximation \tab Approximate linearization or generic replicates (no built-in RWYB mapping) \cr
   Bounded \code{cube}, \code{lpm2}, \code{scps} \tab Exact inclusion probabilities under the method contract \tab Refused \tab Generic \code{subbootstrap} or \code{mrbbootstrap} only (linearization refused) \cr
   Custom methods \tab Declared exact or approximate quality \tab Registered joint support \tab Depends on the variance-family declaration and adapter checks \cr
}


Joint information means \code{\link[=joint_expectation]{joint_expectation()}}, which exposes PPS and
balanced-family quantities. A stage's joint matrix does not cover the
final units of a multistage design. Exact first-order probabilities do not
imply exact variance or confidence-interval coverage, since finite-sample
PPS total estimators can be skewed. \code{vignette("survey-analysis")} shows a
log-scale interval for strictly positive domain totals.
}

\subsection{Linearization with Brewer's approximation}{

Fixed-size PPS stages without replacement (\code{pps_brewer}, \code{pps_systematic},
\code{pps_cps}, \code{pps_sampford}, \code{pps_sps}, \code{pps_pareto}) are linearized by
default with Brewer's approximation (\code{pps = "brewer"} in \code{survey}), which
derives joint inclusion probabilities from the marginal ones (Berger
2004). Brewer names the variance estimator here, not the selection
algorithm, so a Sampford sample gets the same default. It is not an exact
substitute for the joint probabilities, and its accuracy depends on the
design and population. \code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} documents each value of \code{pps}.
}

\subsection{Linearization with joint probabilities}{

A matrix from \code{\link[=joint_expectation]{joint_expectation()}} is exact for CPS, Sampford, systematic
PPS and Poisson selection. Generalized Brewer, SPS, Pareto and
unconstrained cube use the high-entropy approximation (Hajek 1964; Brewer
and Donadio 2003). Exact recursive formulas exist for Brewer's method
(Brewer 2002, ch. 9) but cost \eqn{O(N^3)}{O(N^3)}.

\code{survey} applies a matrix to one stage and reads it by row, so the sample
needs one row per stage-1 unit. A multistage sample is exported at stage 1
with a warning, and one with several rows per stage-1 unit is refused
(\code{samplyr_error_pps_rows_per_psu}). Systematic PPS matrices often hold
zero pair probabilities, which rule out a design-unbiased variance
estimator. A sampled matrix cannot show that every population pair is
positive, so supplying one for systematic PPS warns.
}

\subsection{Replicate weights}{

The generic types (\code{"JK1"}, \code{"JKn"}, \code{"BRR"}, \code{"Fay"}, \code{"bootstrap"},
\code{"subbootstrap"}) resample first-stage units within first-stage strata,
treating a PPS first stage drawn without replacement as drawn with
replacement. That overstates the variance when its sampling fractions are
large (\code{samplyr_warning_replicate_wr_first_stage}). \code{"mrbbootstrap"} and
\code{"rwyb"} read every stage. RWYB supports SRS, draws with replacement,
Poisson selection, and Brewer, CPS, Sampford and systematic PPS, the last
four with approximate joint probabilities and a warning. It is used only for
\code{type = "rwyb"}, never by \code{"auto"}, and needs the svrep package.
Replicates do not recreate ordering, balancing, spatial spreading or hard
constraints, and \code{\link[=as_svrepdesign]{as_svrepdesign()}} states the adapter's limits.

\code{"random_groups"} is the exception. Its replicates are independent samples
of the whole design from \code{execute(reps = R)}, so their spread is a
variance for any selection method, ordering, balancing and systematic
starts included, at a cost of R samples and R - 1 degrees of freedom.
}

\subsection{Systematic stages}{

\code{systematic} stages are linearized with the SRSWOR estimator and
\code{pps_systematic} stages with Brewer's approximation. A systematic design
with interval \eqn{k}{k} has only \eqn{k}{k} distinct samples per stratum,
so its true variance for one frame need not be near either value. Over
repeated draws of 90 per stratum from 1800 with an interval of 20:\tabular{lll}{
   frame order \tab reported / true variance \tab coverage of a 95\% interval \cr
   random \tab 0.73 \tab 90.2\% \cr
   ordered by a trend \tab 1.76 \tab 100.0\% \cr
   period equal to the interval \tab 0.0006 \tab 9.8\% \cr
}


\code{pps_systematic} behaves the same way. Brewer's approximation gave 0.003
of the true variance with 9\% coverage on a frame whose period matched the
interval, and 1.46 on a frame sorted by size. A favorable order is thus
conservative, and a frame resonating with the interval gives intervals
that almost never cover. No replicate type redraws the random start
against the ordered frame, so replicates are also too large when the
order lowers the true variance and too small when it raises it.
Both export functions warn once about these stages, and
\code{systematic_variance = "approximate"} records that the approximation was
accepted. Census stages are exempt.
}

\subsection{Certainty units}{

Under linearization, units with inclusion probability one at a stage
exported with Brewer's treatment form a take-all stratum within the
stage's own strata and parents, however they reached probability one,
balanced designs included. As a census, the stratum contributes no
variance and no degrees of freedom (Cochran 1977, sec. 5.8; Sarndal
et al. 1992, sec. 3.7). Random-size designs keep the Poisson treatment.
The generic replicate types make a certainty PSU its own stratum and
resample its stage-two units, and RWYB gives a certainty unit the factor
one.

Splitting certainty units out can leave a stratum with one probability
unit, whose variance is not estimable. \code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} then warns
(\code{samplyr_warning_lonely_psu}), and \code{options(survey.lonely.psu = "adjust")}
or collapsing strata before export resolves it. RWYB refuses such a
stratum (\code{samplyr_error_rwyb_singleton}) unless the stage is Poisson or
\code{lonely.psu = "certainty"} is given at the final stage.
}

\subsection{One PSU per zone}{

A certainty plan solved with \code{svyplan::n_alloc(n_psu_per_zone = 1)}
draws one PSU from each zone, so no zone has a variance of its own. The
export collapses the zones in the variance groups the plan fixed before
selection, recorded in \code{.pair_1}, which may cross strata (Valliant,
Dever and Kreuter 2018, sec. 15.5.3). One draw per zone has the
with-replacement variance, so the remainder PSUs carry no finite
population correction under linearization, RWYB or the generic replicate
types, while certainty PSUs keep theirs and contribute their later
stages. The collapsed estimator overestimates the variance by a term in
the squared differences between the totals of the zones grouped together
(Wolter 2007, sec. 2.5), most where a group joins zones of different
sizes.
A group of three zones rules out balanced half-samples.
}

\subsection{Selected units with nothing below them}{

A unit accepted by \code{on_empty} with nothing selected under it keeps its
zero in every total. When it is a stage-two or deeper unit inside a
primary unit that still has rows, \code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} warns
(\code{samplyr_warning_export_empty_parent}), because survey computes that
unit's within-parent term without it. This matters only at large
first-stage sampling fractions. When a whole primary unit has no row, the
variance between primary units would lose its zero total and could come
out as zero, so every route refuses it (\code{samplyr_error_export_empty_psu},
and \code{samplyr_error_rwyb_missing_parents} for RWYB).
}

\subsection{Random-size Poisson methods}{

\code{bernoulli} and \code{pps_poisson} select units independently, so the sample
size is random and Brewer's approximation would understate the variance. A
single-stage design, or one with one row per sampled cluster, is exported
with \code{survey::poisson_sampling()}, the Horvitz-Thompson Poisson variance
(Sarndal et al. 1992, sec. 2.8). A multistage design with a Poisson stage,
or a clustered one with several rows per cluster, needs \code{type = "rwyb"}.
Linearization and the generic replicate types refuse it, since they can
lose the variance of the random size.
}

\subsection{Chromy's sequential method}{

\code{pps_chromy} gives each unit \eqn{\lfloor E(n_i) \rfloor}{floor(E(n_i))} or
\eqn{\lceil E(n_i) \rceil}{ceiling(E(n_i))} hits, which is neither
with nor without replacement. Following Chromy (2009), it is exported like a
with-replacement stage, with no FPC and no \code{pps} argument. That
Hansen-Hurwitz treatment can be strongly conservative when the frame order
acts as implicit stratification, but a generalized estimator from Monte
Carlo expected hits was often negative, so it is not offered.
\code{survey::ppsmat()} does not apply, because a PMR matrix's diagonal holds
\eqn{E(n_i^2)}{E(n_i^2)} rather than \eqn{E(n_i)}{E(n_i)}. Chauvet (2019)
gives exact results for the related without-replacement design.
}

\subsection{Balanced, spatial and custom methods}{

Unconstrained \code{cube} is linearized with the high-entropy approximation,
which ignores the balancing. The more closely an outcome follows the
balancing variables, the more the reported variance overstates. In a
simulation the standard error was about five times the true spread for an
outcome correlated 0.98 with the balancing variable, and close to right
for a weakly related one.
Bounded \code{cube}, \code{lpm2} and \code{scps} change pairwise selection beyond that
approximation, so linearization and \code{\link[=joint_expectation]{joint_expectation()}} refuse them, and
\code{type = "subbootstrap"} or \code{"mrbbootstrap"} gives a generic PPS bootstrap
that does not recreate the constraints or the spatial algorithm.

A method registered with \code{sondage::register_method()} can declare a
\code{variance_family}: \code{"srs"} receives the equal-probability treatment,
\code{"pps_brewer"} Brewer's approximation, \code{"poisson"} the Poisson estimator
(and RWYB), \code{"wr"} the with-replacement treatment, and \code{"unsupported"} is
refused by linearization, leaving \code{type = "subbootstrap"}. A random-size
method with no declaration is refused, since its selections are not known
to be independent.
}

\subsection{Two-phase samples}{

\code{\link[survey:twophase]{survey::twophase()}} takes no \code{pps} specification, so a two-phase sample
or a wave whose first phase was drawn with unequal probabilities without
replacement, or by \code{cube}, \code{pps_poisson} or a spatial method, is refused,
although its weights are exact. For a variance, an ultimate-cluster design
treating phase-1 units as drawn with replacement is conservative, at 1.3,
1.8 and 2.1 times the true variance for phase-1 sampling fractions of
1/6, 1/3 and 1/2 with phase 2 drawn inside each phase-1 unit, and 2.4 to
3.6 times with phase 2 drawn across the phase-1 sample.
On other populations the across case reached 4 to 15 times, higher when
little of the outcome's variation lies between phase-1 units.
\code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} gives the call. No two-phase sample has a replicate
export.

Phase 2 is drawn from the phase-1 sample, so its joint inclusion
probabilities are computed there. A single-stage phase 2 drawn with
\code{pps_sampford}, \code{pps_cps}, \code{pps_brewer}, \code{pps_sps} or \code{pps_pareto} exports
with them under \code{method = "full"}, exact for Sampford and CPS and the
high-entropy approximation for the other three. survey reads such a
matrix in the Horvitz-Thompson form, which is unbiased but unstable when
the outcome follows the size measure. In a simulation it was negative in
3.6\% of samples, and 95\% intervals covered 86\%. The export uses the
Sen-Yates-Grundy form for the phase-2 term instead and leaves the phase-1
term as survey computes it. In the same simulation that form was never
negative and covered 93\%. Systematic PPS stays refused, since its zero pair
probabilities bias the variance estimator under any matrix. So are a
multistage phase 2 with an unequal-probability stage and
\code{method = "approx"} or \code{"simple"}, which drop the matrix
(\code{samplyr_error_twophase_phase2_pps}).

A clustered phase 1 with an equal-probability first stage does export,
but its variance is stable only when phase 2 is drawn inside each phase-1
unit. Drawn across them, survey's variance was negative in 26\% to 51\% of
samples and 1.4 to 5 times the true variance otherwise, and the export
warns (\code{samplyr_warning_twophase_across_units}).
}
}
